Consistency would really be simply that anything dropped on an
anti-magic trap is treated as though zapped with cancellation.

This allows for "least surprise" (why do spellbooks blank but not
scrolls?  Why doesn't the /magic/ harp become a normal harp?  etc.) It
presumably also simplifies the code change as most of the necessary
code should already be in there.

In other words, the DevTeam has already made a decision about what it
means to "remove magic" from something, and that's what it is.  Better
to use it than reinvent the wheel.


